SSM integration
===============

The import merge retains both the reconstructed library ancestry and the
existing SSM history. Integration is a new set of changes above that merge;
no historical commits are rewritten or attributed to the integration author.

The Automake library builds the shared implementations used by this SSM tree:
Sample, ChannelHandler, MidiDevice/MidiEvent, WavFile (RiffWav), and Sample's
Allocator support.
NoteTable is the same header-only table in both histories. The remaining
archived sources are distributed but not enabled as new backend dependencies.

Reconciliation choices:
- Sample retains the library allocator and default Mix position, plus SSM's
  IsEmpty state, data granularity and plugin-specific data/sample-type API.
- ChannelHandler retains library string/pointer channels and SSM's ReplaceData
  and corrected pthread_mutex_trylock success test. String transfer is bounded
  to the fixed-size channel buffer.
- MIDI uses the later SSM implementation in namespace spiralcore, retaining
  configured ALSA/OSS selection and bidirectional ALSA support rather than
  reverting to the library snapshot's hard-coded ALSA, single-direction client.
  This does not introduce new MIDI hardware-selection UI or modern backends.
- RiffWav is moved unchanged into the shared library; its existing global WavFile
  API and optional libsndfile/native RIFF paths are preserved.
- Consumers include the library headers directly. The redundant SpiralSound
  directory and libSpiralCommon target have been removed.

Rebuild the host, widget library and every plugin together after regenerating
Autotools files. Namespace-qualified shared types change the C++ ABI; old plugin
binaries are not interchangeable with the rebuilt ones. The host, widgets and
plugins link directly to libspiralcore; libSpiralCommon is no longer built.

Noninteractive regression checks are available with:
    make -C libspiralcore check
They exercise allocator use together with SSM Sample behavior, channel updates,
ReplaceData, and library string/pointer channels. They do not open audio or MIDI
hardware. GUI and hardware interaction are left to the user.

Integration code, tests, documentation and commit text generated or modified
by ChatGPT. Original historical source comments and ancestry are retained.
